  "summary": "A transitional style between Gothic Revival and Queen Anne with exposed structural elements.",
  "description": "Characterized by decorative wooden trim resembling stickwork, board-and-batten siding, and asymmetrical compositions. It emerged as American architects sought to create a uniquely American style while maintaining connections to European traditions.",
  "culturalContext": "Stick Style (1860-1890) — Vincent Scully's named \"transitional\" American style — exposed the building's skeleton as ornament: diagonal stickwork trusses, angle brackets, king posts and trusses patterned across sheathing, as if to declare the timber frame through the wall. Represented by Woodward's First Methodist (Salem), the Emlen House and hundreds of Queen Anne cousins, it linked Gothic Revival honesty to Shingle Style simplification, and remains the American suburb's most carpenter-legible historic style.",
